There is possibly a case that the British might have handled Northern Ireland more sensitivity in the early 70s with a different Home Secretary because Reginald Mordling, who I actually really like, is a truly terrible person to be in charge of the Home Office. So to explain to people who he was, he'd been the runner-up to Ted Heath in the Tory leadership. You should approve of him, Tom, because he had a first in classics from Oxford and he'd been in RAF intelligence during the war. And he's suave, he's witty, he's very liberal-minded for a Tory, he's very clever, he's incredibly affable.

58
00:31:49.000 --> 00:32:24.000
On the other hand, he is luscious, he is incredibly lazy, he is greedy and he is fantastically corrupt. And he's also a massive, massive boozer. So if I tell you his daily intake, you would have brandy for breakfast, gin at mid-morning, and then at lunch he'd have wine and digestif. And then for the rest of the day, he'd just be as the mood took him. I mean, he'd be asleep, actually, for the last of the day. And Reginald Mordling, he's basically one of those people. He's the ultimate sort of 70s British politician, massive glasses.

59
00:32:24.000 --> 00:33:02.000
