60
00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:33.000
What a bloody awful country. So anyway, the story of the next nine months is these men, Heath, mordling, Chichester Clark, basically totally out of their depth, dithering and dallying while the situation gets steadily worse. So a good example of how it's getting worse in a small way. You mentioned the cups of tea. There had been a lot of fraternisation between the army and the locals at the end of 1969. The army had even organised kind of clubs for local kids, trips and stuff for them. They'd organised a New Year's Eve, disdeme, disdeme.

61
00:33:33.000 --> 00:34:02.000
with local girls in dairy. But a year on, things are completely different. Girls who now fraternise with British soldiers are punished for it. There's a soldier in Peter Taylor's book about the army in Northern Ireland, Brits, it's called. Brilliant book. A soldier tells him about finding a girl tied to a lamp post and she had literally been, her head had been shaved and she'd literally been tared and feathered. He said the tar ran down her neck in the front of her breast and her hands were badly affected

62
00:34:02.000 --> 00:34:18.000
where she'd been clawing at her face to get it off and the feathers were stuck to the tar. We had to cut her away from the lamppost and take it to hospital. So this is the sort of context. And then in February 1971, trouble breaks out in an area of North Belfast called the New Lodge area.

63
00:34:18.000 --> 00:34:36.000
And this is a Catholic area. It's very densely populated. And the Army believed that the provisional have been stockpiling guns on the estate and have been launching search missions. There are days of clashes and these escalates on the evening of the 5th of February. into street fighting, the army sent in a unit of Royal Artillery.

64
00:34:36.000 --> 00:35:12.000
They get there, it's chaotic scene, bricks, petrol bombs, bottles, all of this. And then as one soldier described it, it's like the crowd parts, like the sort of parting of the Red Sea. As he describes it, this chap just popped out with the machine gun and opened up. And this chap is Billy Reed of the Provisional IRA. He's got a sterling submachine gun. He shoots four soldiers wounded, and he shoots Gunner Robert Curtis through the heart. And Curtis becomes the first British soldier to die in Northern Ireland. He was from Newcastle.

65
00:35:12.000 --> 00:35:30.000
He was 20 years old. He left a pregnant wife who was also 20. An interview by the press afterwards, his father said, I have no idea what my son died for. And his widow, his wife, said, Our boys should be brought home immediately. We should leave the mobs to fight it out. among themselves.

66
00:35:30.000 --> 00:36:09.000
Now, already at this point, a lot of people in Britain agree with this. So if you look at the polls, in 1969, most people had favoured the decision to introduce troops, but even in early, you know, the early 1970s, so 70, 71, more than half of the electorate think that the troops should be brought home and Northern Ireland should just be abandoned. And this is what the government is kind of contending with all through this story. But Heath's cabinet have absolutely no intention of pulling out. out. So if you look at the Cabinet minutes after this, they've lost their first, you know, British Army casualty. And the Cabinet minutes say, they know, and I quote, many people would

67
00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:44.000
favor abandoning the province to its fate, a course which the government could not contemplate. And the reason they couldn't contemplate is they say international opinion would damn us for it. I mean, Ted Kennedy wouldn't, but a lot of other people would. And the result would be the sectarian bloodbath. And they agree a common line. And they say, this is not. It's not just about communal strife. It's not about rebellion against political injustice. This is the outcome of deliberate terrorism by the IRA. Now, through this story, we have been quite down on British government policy,

68
00:36:44.000 --> 00:37:29.000
and we will probably continue to be, because Heath goes on to make some absolutely cataclysmic mistakes. But I think it is actually worth stopping, just to say for a moment that he's not entirely wrong about this. So the conflict obviously has deep roots, and among the causes, undoubtedly, are British government neglect and incompetence and, of course, the discriminatory nature of the storm on state. But just on the British side, Heath's intentions, I think, are pretty admirable. Heath wants to see a reformed, non-sectarian, Northern Irish state that does not discriminate. everybody's treated equally and nobody gets shot.

69
00:37:29.000 --> 00:37:53.000
And actually he's open to all kinds of different things. He's open to links with Dublin. He's open to rethinking Northern Ireland's constitutional status. He will consider anything. And historians who've written about this almost uniformly say, the Heath government is much more open-minded when it comes to Northern Ireland than any previous British government had ever been. Now, as for the army, the army made terrible mistakes

